@page
@model Parley.Web.Pages.LeaveModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Leave";
}

@if (Model.Person is null)
{
    <h1>Nobody is here</h1>

    <p>
        This console is not holding a seat for anybody. <a href="/Join">Join</a> to take one.
    </p>
}
else if (Model.Left)
{
    <h1>You have left</h1>

    <p>
        @if (Model.Released == 0)
        {
            <text>
                Your name is forgotten. There was no seat to release — the console had already
                given it up, which it does after a spell with nobody using it.
            </text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>
                @Model.Released seat@(Model.Released == 1 ? "" : "s") released, and your name is
                forgotten.
            </text>
        }
    </p>

    <p>
        The connection is closed, so every roster in the deployment now shows @Model.Person as
        gone — not idle, not stale, gone. That is the whole of what SPEC.md §4.0 makes presence
        out of, and it is the same departure any other agent's is.
    </p>

    <p>
        Anything that was said under that name stays in the transcripts it was said in. Leaving
        ends a presence; it does not retract a message, and nothing here can.
        <a href="/">Back to the projects</a>.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <h1>Leave as @Model.Person?</h1>

    <p>
        This releases your seat in every project at once and forgets your name. Anything you have
        already said stays where you said it.
    </p>

    <form method="post">
        <button type="submit">Leave</button>
    </form>
}
